  "query_preview": "..A weak shortwave trough, currently clipping the northwestern edge\nof the state, is leading an increase in mid-level clouds in \nnorthern AZ and will help support a slight chance of showers and \nthunderstorms, mainly over the higher terrain through through \nThursday before moving off to east. The ridge of high pressure \nextending across southwestern AZ, continue to support the overall\ndry and seasonably warm conditions through Friday, and is helping\nto limit activity from the shortwave trough. The pressure \ngradient between the two features is supporting easterly to \nsoutheasterly winds during the daytime, around 5 to 10 mph.\n\nThe ridge begins to break down quickly over the weekend, with \nsouthwesterly winds developing. Ensemble model forecasts show the\npotential for a deepening trough over the interior Pacific \nNorthwest around Friday or early Saturday and then dives \nsouthward around Sunday and into early next week. Depending on the\ntiming of when this trough develops and deepens, it could pull \nmoisture from Hurricane Rachel, which is currently moving west \nfurther into the Pacific Ocean and will weaken over the next \nseveral days.\n\nWhile model ensembles have trended toward tropical moisture being\ncaptured by the trough and pushed into Arizona this weekend or\nearly next week, potentially creating some impactful weather, \nthere is still roughly a 20-30 percent chance that the timing of \nwhen the trough and Hurricane Rachel interact could limit the \namount to tropical moisture captured and pulled into the region. \nFor now continue to monitor the forecast and any future \ncommunication about the weather for this weekend or early next \nweek.",
